---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About Best OutDoor Wears"
  description="Best OutDoor Wears teaches outdoor survival and safety skills to beginners — plainly, without the gear catalogue, and without pretending a card can replace training."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose legal-page" style="max-width:74ch; padding-top:20px;">
      <h1>About Best OutDoor Wears</h1>
      <p>
        Most outdoor survival content falls into one of two piles. Either it is written for people who already
        know what they are doing — friction fire, debris huts, things that make good videos and rarely make the
        difference — or it is a kit list, because kit is easy to sell. Neither pile talks much about what
        actually goes wrong, which is a run of small, reasonable decisions made hours before anything felt like
        an emergency.
      </p>
      <h2>What we focus on</h2>
      <p>
        The unglamorous parts: telling someone your plan, honouring a turnaround time, getting out of the wind
        before you are cold, and knowing that food is the last thing to worry about. We built
        <a href={url('/product/')}>Survival Cards</a> because reading this material does not make it stick —
        doing it once in your garden does, and arguing about it round a fire does better.
      </p>
      <h2>What we will not do</h2>
      <p>
        We do not invent numbers. Where the honest answer is "it depends on where you are", we say that
        instead of printing a figure that sounds authoritative. We do not teach wild-food identification,
        because no card can make that safe. And we do not claim the deck replaces a first aid course — it is a
        prompt for people who have done one.
      </p>
      <h2>Questions?</h2>
      <p>Reach out any time through our <a href={url('/contact/')}>contact page</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
